@CoronelBuendia , I have created a lighter version of the plotting module (see **ivanmaione/merge_2D_3D_CAD_plot_light**) that has, more or less, the same user interface of that in this PR but, as discusses, implement a "simpler" internal code, but less "flexible" for integration of new plot or display APIs. I think the new branch is a good "middle way" between our view of this module :)

Not sure if in the tutorial we should stress on the fact that the "preferred" way to plot something should be through the display.* (plot_2d, plot_3d, show_cad) functions (apart for the component that has its own component.* function). For the moment, the tutorial is more focused on the use of Plotters that, as developer, I find more useful since they store also the options and data to be plotted. However one has always to choose the right plotter for the right object that, maybe, could be annoying... 

In any case, I would appreciate it if you and @DanShort12 could check both implementations and decide which one should be merged in bluemira (for me it's the same at the moment). In case the lighter version is chosen, could one of you please take care of merging it in the branch of this PR? Thanks.

> I read somewhere recently that you should avoid using Any for your typing hints. When we maybe later test things based on type some things slip through whereas if you are strict you can always make it less strict.
> 
> For now it may be easier to go with it, just thought I'd bring it up

It's a bit more complicated than that, as a build config may end up being nested:

```python
{
    "A Build": {
        "variable": 1, 
        "more_config": {
            "another_var": 2,
            "something": "spam",
        }
    }
}
```

I think what it really needs is a recursive type, which should be doable with a forward declaration:

```python
BuildConfig = Dict[str, Union[int, float, str, "BuildConfig"]]
```

